WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.680
When the new Mac Mini came out in October,
it made a lot of buzz because Apple packed

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.120
much better hardware than anyone was expecting.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.379
The RAM is once again user-upgradable, even
though it's not easy, it has a very impressive

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:18.820
4 thunderbolts 3 ports, the option for 10-gigabit
ethernet, and up to a 6 core i7 processor.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.000
It was basically a Mac Mini Pro.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.330
The six-core i7 CPU outperformed any iMac
back then, since they were limited to 4 cores,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:33.030
and with the promise of optimized eGPU support
with Mojave’s new prefer eGPU feature, it

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.300
was a fantastic value!

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.980
And we’d still recommend everyone to get
the $1,100 Mac Mini over the $800 Mac Mini

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:45.610
because you get the 6-core CPU and a 256GB
SSD, which is the least we’d recommend on

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:46.770
any computer.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.970
And now that it’s getting close to a year
later, has the Mac Mini lived up to the potential

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.260
we first saw in it?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.350
No.. and I’ll tell you exactly why in just
a minute.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.829
There are a couple of things that are really
special about the Mac Mini, and the biggest

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:04.869
thing is the extensive variety of I/O, with
4 Thunderbolt 3 ports, 2 USB 3 ports, the

00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:07.650
option of 10GB Ethernet, and HDMI.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:13.490
You literally cannot get this much I/O on
any other mac other than the $5,000 iMac Pro.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:19.939
It even allows you to swap out the RAM yourself
and save about $550 on a 64GB kit compared

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:25.759
to Apple’s $1,000 64GB RAM upgrade by using
the link in the description below.

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:30.079
The Mac Mini packs a ton of performance for
the compact size, and the most important thing

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:35.600
is that it runs MacOS and will continue to
get free macOS updates for years to come.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.979
Because of the compact size, you’re definitely
not getting the full performance out of the

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.150
six-core CPU because it does throttle down
a little bit compared to its potential in

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.700
a machine with better cooling, but it still
performs very well.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.759
The only major downside of the Mac Mini is
that it doesn’t pack a dedicated graphics

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:55.280
chip, but that may not be a bad thing if you’re
someone like a photographer or programmer

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.539
who literally doesn’t need any extra graphics
performance at all.

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:02.280
But if you’re looking to do some video editing
or 3D animation work, you’re definitely

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.049
gonna want to hook up an eGPU.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:10.130
We recommend the Razer Core X eGPU enclosure
with a Vega 64 graphics card for around $700,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:15.870
but you can definitely buy an RX 580 instead
and bring that down to around $500.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.910
In our previous testing from a few months
ago, we noticed that it shot graphics performance

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.180
through the roof, so it seems like an awesome
deal for a very capable machine.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.550
We recently got a new video editing in our
office, and she’s been using a Mac Mini

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.250
that we got in as a loaner for a video as
a temporary editing setup because we already

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.820
have everything we need for it, including
a 4K monitor, a wireless mouse, an old Apple

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.020
keyboard and an eGPU that we used for previous
videos.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.260
However, even though we already have everything
we need to make this Mac Mini shine, we’ll

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.430
be sending it back and won’t be getting
another one for long-term use.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.430
Here’s why.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:56.530
The iMac has just been updated for 2019, and
even the 21.5” 4K iMacs now come with the

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:02.970
same 6-core processors, and you can eve get
8-core i9 processors on the MacBooks and 5K

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:03.970
iMacs.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.360
And while it may seem that the Mac Mini is
a much better deal, now that the iMacs have

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.650
been updated, everything that’s bundled
in for that price makes it a MUCH better value

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.480
than the Mac Mini, even if you have to spend
a little bit more.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.660
First off, the Mac Mini comes with nothing.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.660
No mouse.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.660
No Keyboard.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:20.660
And No display.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.260
While the iMac comes with all of that.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.440
That’s $100 for the keyboard.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.930
$79 for the mouse.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:30.200
And the best part is the 5K display, which
is one of the best displays you can find on

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.340
the market, featuring incredible color accuracy,
brightness and super sharp resolution.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.850
The standalone LG version of that display
was priced at $1,300.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:44.310
So if you add all of that up, the iMac is
such a better deal, and it comes with dedicated

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.430
graphics already.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.390
The problem with getting an eGPU on the Mac
Mini is that you lose a lot of performance

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:54.300
efficiency because the data has to go through
the Thunderbolt 3 cable, so a much more powerful

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:00.020
Vega 64 graphics card ends up being not that
much faster than the Radeon Pro 580X in the

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.020
5K iMac.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.500
Now of course, if you don’t need graphics
performance, the Mac Mini starts to make a

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.590
lot of sense.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.400
But if you’re a photographer who is going
to buy a very color accurate 4K display for

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.420
a few hundred dollars.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:16.180
It actually makes more sense to just pay $500
more to get a very color accurate P3 display,

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:21.959
mouse, keyboard and 560X graphics, which is
what comes in the $2800 MacBook Pro.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:27.370
Now if you’re looking for a bit more performance
than that with the i7 six-core processor,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:34.000
512GB SSD and you already have your own mouse,
keyboard, and monitor, you’ll spend $2,000

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.130
if you get an eGPU with the RX 580 graphics.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:42.841
For just $400 more, you can get the top-end
5K iMac which comes with peripherals, much

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:47.370
better graphics performance due to it being
mora efficient inside the machine, easy access

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:53.370
to RAM with double the RAM slots, and of course,
that gorgeous and color accurate 5K display.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:58.620
Even if you spend more and get a Vega 64 graphics
card in the eGPU, in many cases, the 580X

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.840
in the iMac will perform just as well or even
better and you don’t need to have a large

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.840
and loud box sitting on your desk.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.350
Now if Apple upgraded the Mac Mini with an
8-core processor, or if eGPU performance was

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.380
better, it would make a lot more sense.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.000
But at this time, we would honestly suggest
almost everyone to just save up a bit more

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.980
cash and go for the new iMac.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:21.460
The experience will be much better and you’ll
get more performance for the cash.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.160
Now we told you that there are a few cases
where the Mac Mini still makes a lot of sense.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.910
The biggest one is if you are using it as
a server or in other cases you don’t even

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.640
need a display most of the time, just a nice
and small computer.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.030
The Mac Mini would be a perfect option for
this.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:38.930
The second is if you really need more than
two thunderbolts 3 ports, which you can’t

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.860
get on the new iMac, except for the iMac Pro.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.200
Along with that, if you need 10GB ethernet
and you don’t want to use up a Thunderbolt

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.420
3 port for an adapter, it’s only a $100
option on the Mac Mini.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:55.000
Lastly, the Mac Mini makes sense if you don’t
need a good graphics card, say for photo editing

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.160
or coding, and you already have a display
and peripherals that you want to use.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:04.100
For most people though, even in this scenario,
we would highly suggest giving the 4K or 5K

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.240
iMac a shot, its seriously amazing once you
try it.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.110
We’ve been using the 5K iMac for years,
and we can’t go back to average 4K monitors.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:16.360
The Mac Mini isn’t a bad machine by any
means, and it still has some good uses, but

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.090
almost a year later, it doesn’t offer the
same value that it had at launch.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:24.970
The processors are no longer special, considering
that the new MacBook Pro and 5K iMac now offer

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:29.450
8-cores, and eGPU performance is nowhere near
where we thought it would be.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:33.130
We really hope that Apple won’t forget about
this tiny powerhouse and will keep updating

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:37.120
it with new processors and better cooling,
but for now, we think most people will have

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:50.160
a much better experience with an iMac.

